Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Spruce Needles Siphon the Low-End
- Story & Context: When a severe ice storm froze the mechanical foghorn at Cape Nordkinn in mid-December 1978, Astrid Holm sprinted eighty steps up the iron tower with her BBb tuba, smelling burnt circuit grease as the trawler Havørn drifted blind toward the reefs. Shoving damp spruce cuttings into the brass flare to dull the sharp attack, she leaned into the transmitter mic and played deep, rhythmic half-time pulses through an oscillating tape delay, using the immense sub-octaves to guide the vessel into the bay. Radio operators across the frozen channel tuned their shortwave sets to the strange acoustic pulse, catching her low, reassuring voice murmured over the tape hiss: "Hold your heading toward the hum."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Moody macro shot of green frost-rimed spruce needles wedged inside the tarnished bell of a brass tuba, backlit by the warm amber filament of an old vacuum tube, icy condensation dripping down curved metal in deep navy and honey-gold light.

Track 2: Did the Root-Graft Take?
- Story & Context: Confined to the damp cellar of Isfjord Radio during sixty straight days of polar dark, Einar Lind tended a makeshift glass cold-frame where he attempted to graft hardy cloudberry roots onto winter tomato shoots. To keep the cellar's air from stagnating, he aimed Astrid's recorded tuba drones through a hollowed spruce pipe into the soil bed, confessing to listening trawler skippers that the 50Hz brass vibrations were the only thing keeping the young stems alive. In the small hours of January, offshore crews listened to the soft crunch of his garden trowel over cozy, swinging tape delays as Einar whispered into the open frequency: "Bury the marrow where the cold won't dig."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Intimate painterly shot of delicate green seedlings sprouting from dark damp potting soil inside an antique glass greenhouse frame, reflected in the polished brass tubing of a low horn resting against damp wooden planks in soft, golden dawn-light.

Track 3: Solveig’s Iron Greenhouse
- Story & Context: Long after the Bear Island weather outpost was automated, coastal fishermen perpetuated the legend of Solveig’s Iron Greenhouse, an abandoned glass conservatory bolted to the black coastal cliffs. Skippers adrift in dense pack ice swore that when their diesel engines idled, their hull plates would resonate with phantom tuba skanks drifting from an unpowered battery terminal left inside the ruin. Coast guards investigating the rumor found only cracked glass panes, wild Arctic moss creeping over rotting carpet, and an endless cassette loop turning on solar power, humming a ghostly melody under a scratched voice: "The glass will crack before the garden sleeps."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Haunting cinematic view of a weathered Victorian iron-framed greenhouse perched on a dark basalt cliff over a misty polar ocean, illuminated from within by a soft amber lantern glow that shines through cracked, salt-caked glass panes into swirling sea mist.

